{
  "dom_id": "step-q315933",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14274523/assets/f17ce467f5bff34f0c04326d1a23ca8621f459571be8c98a745174a3d5587337.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=100$ units. Determine the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the largest possible area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} units",
  "worked_solution": "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $100$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{100-2r}{r}=\\frac{100}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{100}{r}-2) \\\\  & =50r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=50-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 50-2r & =0 \\\\ -2r & =-50 \\\\ r & =\\frac{(-50)}{(-2)} \\\\ r & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=25$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(25)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(25)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=25$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
